=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 20 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 20: Abraham and Abimelech
sub: She Is My Sister • God Came to Abimelech in a Dream • The Father of the Faithful Struggled Too
refs: Genesis 20:1-18
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== She is my sister
--- She Is My Sister
kicker: vv. 1–2
sub: Sojourning in Gerar
scripture: Genesis 20:1-2 | mark: She is my sister
p: **Genesis 20** tells a familiar story, Abraham and Sarah lying about being brother and sister while sojourning in another nation. We were just reading a similar account in **Genesis 12**, where they went to Egypt to flee a famine, acting out of fear, and compounding his sin, Abraham lied to Pharaoh about being married to fair Sarah. Here, they sojourn in Gerar, a nearby kingdom and their ruler, Abimelech sent and took Sarah, believing she was not married to Abraham. [[lesson:genesis-12-the-call-of-abram|Genesis 12]]

=== God came to Abimelech
--- God Came to Abimelech in a Dream
kicker: vv. 3–5
sub: In the Integrity of My Heart
scripture: Genesis 20:3-5 | mark: God came to Abimelech in a dream; in the integrity of my heart and innocency of my hands
p: Now, when Abraham is outed by Abimelech, whom God visited in a dream to reveal the truth of Abraham's lie, Abimelech is wroth with anger, because he had taken Sarah *in the integrity of my heart and innocency of my hands*.

--- I Withheld Thee from Sinning
kicker: vv. 6–7
sub: He Is a Prophet
scripture: Genesis 20:6-7 | mark: for I also withheld thee from sinning against me
p: He was actually a God-fearing king, of a righteous nation. He believed God would have killed him had He not kept the king from knowing Sarah, and committing adultery.

--- What Hast Thou Done unto Us?
kicker: vv. 8–10
sub: Deeds That Ought Not to Be Done
scripture: Genesis 20:8-10 | mark: What hast thou done unto us?
p: He was righteously angry at Abraham for putting himself and his nation at risk.

--- God Fearing, Not Righteous
kicker: Application • Compare scripture
sub: Had to Think about That for a Minute
p: Oh, and I did what I just warned against in believing everything a human says in the Bible as truth, Abimelech and his Nation were not righteous, as in saved believers, just because he said so. Their nation is listed as being near Sodom and Gomorrah and clearly if you're kidnapping women passing through your land and making them your wife, forcing yourself upon them, you're not doing right by God.
p: But it's clear that Abimelech was God fearing. That he understood the real God, His laws, and knew the curses that would come upon him for committing adultery in that manner.
p: Had to think about that for a minute.

=== A half-truth
--- She Is the Daughter of My Father
kicker: vv. 11–12
sub: Surely the Fear of God Is Not in This Place
scripture: Genesis 20:11-12 | mark: she is the daughter of my father, but not the daughter of my mother
p: When confronted, Abraham says essentially, well, I was telling a half-truth, we are married but she's my half sister. Daughter of his father Terah and a different mother.

--- This Is Thy Kindness
kicker: v. 13
sub: At Every Place Whither We Shall Come
big: Genesis 20:13 | mark: at every place whither we shall come, say of me, He is my brother
p: We learn that this tactic, lying, wasn't a one-off, or even a two-off.

--- Told under Duress
kicker: Application • Compare scripture
sub: Not Enough to Draw a Definitive Conclusion
p: Now this is held dogmatically by many, that Abraham and Sarah were half-siblings, sharing the same father. But this is the only instance where this appears in scripture, and it's Abraham caught in one lie by an angry king, told under duress. We cannot assume she is Terah's daughter just because Abraham says it. Unless God or someone in the Spirit speaking for God says it, we have to look at context to see if what they're saying is true, or the truth from their perspective, or possibly a lie or rationalization.
p: But, Abraham does make the claim. It is possible that he's telling the truth, but it's not enough to draw a definitive conclusion.

--- Righteous Abraham
kicker: Application • The father of the faithful
sub: He Struggled with Sin and Faith, Repeatedly
p: Righteous Abraham. Father to us all who believe on Christ Jesus. Who with perfect faith believed God would spare or raise Isaac, whom he was prepared to sacrifice on an altar by faith. Great Abraham. And he struggled with sin and faith, repeatedly.
p: He lies, the Bible says, because she is fair (even elderly in years), and he believes that these nations he's sojourning in are godless heathens who would kill him as husband to take his wife. He leaves the land God gave him out of fear. In **Genesis 12**, fear of famine. And he concocts a lie about Sarah out of fear. And, at least twice, it bites him in the butt and God has to bail him out.

--- A Friend of God
kicker: Application • The father of the faithful
sub: We Can Know the Same Is True of Us
p: This should be a great lesson for us, especially those of us who aren't as righteous as Abraham. If he, a mighty man of faith, called "a friend of God," struggled with sin and fear. Who is embarrassingly caught in the same lie more than once, recorded forever in the preserved word of God, then we can know the same is true of us.
p: We all have sin in our lives that we struggle with. It is different for each person, and at various times in our life. Some old sins we conquer with the help of God's word and the Spirit within, while others we may pick up along the way.

--- All Have Sinned
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 20:13
fig: Romans 3:23 | Romans 3:23
fig: 1 John 1:8 | 1 John 1:8
callout: We shouldn't be puffed up in our righteousness, hypocritically fruit inspecting other believing Christians to compare our own righteousness against theirs. Because we all sin and our righteousness is as filthy rags, the Bible says. But by grace are we saved, and the imputed righteousness of Jesus Christ covers us all.

--- Judge While Understanding Our Own Failings
kicker: Application • The father of the faithful
sub: Mercy and Grace
p: This doesn't mean to not judge, but to judge while understanding our own failings. Showing mercy and grace as God has shown us.
p: If Abraham struggled with fear and dishonesty, at certain times and situations, understand that we too likewise struggle.

=== God healed Abimelech
--- He Is Reproved
kicker: vv. 14–16
sub: A Thousand Pieces of Silver
scripture: Genesis 20:14-16 | mark: thus she was reproved
p: But we can also see that God saves us regardless of our fear or lack of faith.

--- Abraham Prayed unto God
kicker: vv. 17–18
sub: God Healed Abimelech
scripture: Genesis 20:17-18 | mark: So Abraham prayed unto God: and God healed Abimelech
p: He chastises those he loves and scourges every son. He often will expose us to embarrassment and humiliation in exposing our sin. But it is to our benefit, as it is here for Abraham.

--- Compare Scripture
center: Again it's important to compare scripture to see if something is so.
